## 3. Build system decision

**Swift Package Manager + Command Line Tools, with a script that assembles the `.app` bundle.**

| Option | Effect |
|---|---|
| Xcode project + `xcodebuild` | Needs Xcode license acceptance (`sudo`), hand-written `project.pbxproj`, hosted unit tests would launch the real menu bar app |
| **SwiftPM + bundle script** | Works today with CLT, diffable text-only config, `swift test` runs core tests hostless (no risk of touching real displays), opens fine in Xcode via `Package.swift` |

- `swift build -c release --product DisplayManager --scratch-path <build dir>`.
- `Scripts/build-app.sh` builds and assembles:
  `DisplayManager.app/Contents/{MacOS/DisplayManager, Info.plist, PkgInfo, Resources/AppIcon.icns}`,
  stamps version into Info.plist, runs `plutil -lint`, ad-hoc signs, `codesign --verify --strict`.
- Build directory defaults to `~/Library/Caches/DisplayManager-build` (overridable with `DM_BUILD_DIR`).
  Keeps build products out of the iCloud Drive repo.
- Toolchain selection in scripts: use `xcrun swift` if it works. If it fails (for example the license error) and
  CLT exists, export `DEVELOPER_DIR=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools` for the script process only.
  No system changes, no `sudo`.
- App icon: `Scripts/generate-icon.swift` draws a simple original icon (two monitor shapes) with CoreGraphics,
  then `sips` + `iconutil` produce `Packaging/AppIcon.icns`. Generated once and committed.
  SF Symbols are not used for the app icon (their license forbids icons/logos). The menu bar uses the
  `display` SF Symbol, which is allowed UI use.

---

## 4. Architecture

### 4.1 Layers

```
┌──────────────── DisplayManager (executable: AppKit, SwiftUI, IOKit, CoreGraphics, Carbon, ServiceManagement) ─────────────┐
│ AppDelegate → AppModel (@MainActor @Observable, the single UI state)                                                        │
│   StatusItemController ─ left click: NSMenu      right click / ctrl-click: System Settings › Displays                        │
│   WindowCoordinator ─ Layout Manager window, Settings window (NSWindow + NSHostingController)                                │
│   AutomationCoordinator ─ events → debounce → stable snapshot → decide (Core) → ApplyCoordinator                             │
│   ApplyCoordinator ─ serial queue of apply requests, uses Core.LayoutApplier                                                 │
│   ProfileStore (JSON file) · Preferences (UserDefaults) · DiagnosticsLog (os.Logger + ring buffer)                           │
│   System adapters: DisplayplacerClient (Process) · CGDisplayReader · PortTopologyReader (IOKit) · SnapshotService            │
│                    DisplayEventMonitor · LoginItemController · HotKeyCenter · SystemSettingsOpener                           │
└───────────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                                │ Sendable value types only
┌───────────────────────────────── DisplayManagerCore (library, Foundation only, unit-tested) ──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Models · ScreenSpecCodec · ListOutputParser · CommandBuilder · ExecutableLocator · ApplyOutcome classifier                    │
│ EDID · PortTransportRecord · SnapshotAssembler (join) · DisplayResolver · LayoutComparator · RuleMatcher                     │
│ AutomationPolicy (trigger decision, loop guard, circuit breaker) · LayoutApplier (bounded retry + verify, protocol-driven)     │
│ LayoutDocument (versioned) · Migrator · ImportValidator/Merger · ShortcutValidator · PreviewGeometry · DisplayManagerError    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

Rules:
- Core has no AppKit, IOKit, CoreGraphics or Process calls. Hardware readers produce plain records that Core consumes.
- Only two small protocols exist for testability: `DisplayControl` (list + apply) and `Sleeper` (injectable delay)
  used by `LayoutApplier`. No other DI layers.
- All models are `Codable, Hashable, Sendable` structs.

### 4.2 Why AppKit lifecycle instead of SwiftUI `App`/`MenuBarExtra`

- `MenuBarExtra` cannot distinguish left and right clicks.
- Opening the SwiftUI `Settings` scene from AppKit code is unreliable on macOS 14+ (`showSettingsWindow:` is
  deprecated; `openSettings` needs a SwiftUI view context and hidden-window workarounds).
- AppKit lifecycle (`main.swift` → `NSApplication` + `AppDelegate`) gives full control over `NSStatusItem`,
  window activation and ordering. SwiftUI is still used for all window content.
- A minimal `NSApp.mainMenu` (App: About, Settings… ⌘,, Quit ⌘Q; Edit: Undo/Redo/Cut/Copy/Paste/Select All;
  Window: Close ⌘W, Minimize ⌘M) is installed so text fields support copy/paste and ⌘W works in an accessory app.

### 4.3 Concurrency

- UI state and coordinators: `@MainActor`.
- `DisplayplacerClient`: runs `Process` off the main actor; stdout and stderr are drained concurrently to avoid
  pipe deadlock; timeout 20 s → `terminate()`, then `SIGKILL` after 2 s.
- C callbacks (CG reconfiguration, IOKit notifications, Carbon hotkeys) receive an `Unmanaged` context and hop to
  the main actor with `Task { @MainActor in … }`. No `assumeIsolated` on callback paths.
- Apply requests are serialized: one apply at a time; a newer request replaces any queued request; a running
  `displayplacer` process is never killed mid-apply (except timeout).

