## 9. displayplacer integration

### 9.1 Discovery (`ExecutableLocator`, pure logic with injected `isExecutable`)

Order (first executable wins, symlinks resolved for display):
1. User override path from Settings (`Choose…`), if set and executable.
2. `/opt/homebrew/bin/displayplacer`
3. `/usr/local/bin/displayplacer`
4. Each directory in the process `PATH` (launchd gives GUI apps a minimal PATH), plus
   `~/.local/bin`, `~/bin`, `/opt/local/bin` (MacPorts).

No login-shell execution (it runs user rc files). Version: `displayplacer --version` → parse `displayplacer v1.4.0`.
Minimum supported: **1.4.0** (list format changed in 1.4.0). Older → "Unsupported displayplacer version, run
`brew upgrade displayplacer`". Re-checked at launch, when Settings opens, and before an apply if the cached path vanished.
Never installs anything. Missing → Settings shows `displayplacer not found`, the install command
`brew install displayplacer` with a Copy button, and a link to the project page.

### 9.2 Parsing `displayplacer list` (v1.4.0)

```
Persistent screen id: <UUID>
Contextual screen id: <Int>
Serial screen id: s<Int>
Type: <free text>                       e.g. "MacBook built in screen", "27 inch external screen"
Resolution: <W>x<H>
Hertz: <Int>
Color Depth: <Int>
Scaling: on|off
Origin: (<x>,<y>)[ - main display]
Rotation: <deg>[ - <hint text>]
Enabled: true|false
Resolutions for rotation <deg>:
  mode <n>: res:<W>x<H> hz:<Int> color_depth:<Int>[ scaling:on][ <-- current mode]
<blank>
…next screen…
Execute the command below to set your screens to the current arrangement. …
<blank>
displayplacer "id:<…> res:… hz:… color_depth:… enabled:true scaling:on origin:(x,y) degree:0" "id:…"
```

- **Authoritative configuration = the final `displayplacer "…" "…"` line.** It is exactly what displayplacer accepts,
  including mirror sets (`id:A+B`) and disabled screens. Split with a quote-aware tokenizer.
- Per-screen blocks provide metadata: persistent/contextual/serial ids, type, main flag, supported modes (for the
  unsupported-resolution pre-check), enabled.
- Parsing is label-based; unknown labels and unknown `key:value` tokens are kept (`extraArguments`) and re-emitted,
  for forward compatibility.
- Missing command line, no screens, or unparseable required token → `malformedListOutput` with a raw snippet in diagnostics.

### 9.3 Screen spec model and command generation

```swift
struct ScreenSetting: Codable, Hashable, Sendable {
    var displayIDs: [String]      // 1 id, or several for a mirror set (first = "optimize for")
    var enabled: Bool
    var resolution: Size?         // points, as displayplacer reports (nil when disabled)
    var refreshRate: Int?         // hz
    var colorDepth: Int?
    var scaling: Bool?
    var origin: Point?            // global coordinates, main display at (0,0), y grows downward
    var rotation: Int?            // 0/90/180/270
    var extraArguments: [String: String]
}
```

- Emitted token order matches displayplacer's own: `id res hz color_depth enabled scaling origin degree` + extras.
  Disabled screen → `id:<ids> enabled:false`.
- At apply time ids are **re-resolved** to the currently connected displays (section 11.1), so the command uses the
  current persistent ids even if macOS swapped them.
- Arguments go to `Process.arguments` one element per screen. No shell, so paths with spaces are safe.
- Character whitelist for every token (ids `[A-Za-z0-9-+]`, numbers, `(`, `)`, `,`, `x`, `:`). Anything else is rejected.
  This protects the copyable raw command from injection via imported files.
- **Raw command** (Show / Copy): `'<path>' "<spec>" "<spec>"`. Path single-quoted only when needed. Label says whether
  ids were resolved against connected displays or are the saved ids (some displays not connected).

### 9.4 Execution and outcome classification

`DisplayplacerClient` implements `DisplayControl { listLayout(); apply(arguments:) }` returning
`ProcessOutput { exitCode, stdout, stderr, duration, timedOut }`.

| Observation | Classification | Retryable |
|---|---|---|
| exit 0 | success (then verify) | n/a |
| exit ≠ 0, output contains `Unable to find screen` | `displayNotFound` | yes (display may be waking) |
| exit ≠ 0, output mentions a missing resolution/mode | `unsupportedMode` | no |
| exit ≠ 0, `Argument parsing error` | `invalidArguments` (bug or bad import) | no |
| timeout | `timedOut` | yes (once) |
| launch failure (ENOENT, permission) | `executableUnavailable` | no, re-run discovery |
| other exit ≠ 0 | `failed(exitCode)` | yes |

Exit code plus verification is the ground truth. Output text only refines the message.

---

## 10. Persistence model

### 10.1 Locations

- Profiles, rules, learned ports: `~/Library/Application Support/DisplayManager/layouts.json`
  (pretty, sorted keys, ISO 8601 dates, atomic write after each change).
- Preferences: `UserDefaults` (`autoApplyEnabled` = true, `reapplyAfterWake` = true, `settleDelaySeconds` = 2.0,
  `displayplacerPathOverride`, `hasCompletedFirstLaunch`).
- Launch at Login: not stored; always read from `SMAppService.mainApp.status`.
- Backups: before a migration or a "Replace All" import → `layouts.backup-<timestamp>.json` (keep last 5).
- Unreadable/invalid file at launch → moved to `layouts.corrupt-<timestamp>.json`, user alerted, app starts empty.
  The original is never overwritten silently.

### 10.2 Document format (also the import/export format)

```json
{
  "version": 1,
  "exportedBy": "DisplayManager 1.0.0",
  "profiles": [{
    "id": "UUID", "name": "Desk", "createdAt": "…", "updatedAt": "…",
    "screens": [{ "displayIDs": ["1D8D03AE-…"], "enabled": true,
                  "resolution": {"width": 3008, "height": 1692}, "refreshRate": 60, "colorDepth": 8,
                  "scaling": true, "origin": {"x": 0, "y": 0}, "rotation": 0, "extraArguments": {} }],
    "displays": [{ "persistentID": "1D8D03AE-…", "contextualID": 3, "serialID": "s19",
                   "vendorID": 25001, "productID": 10145, "serialNumber": 19,
                   "name": "Mi Monitor", "typeDescription": "27 inch external screen", "isBuiltIn": false,
                   "bounds": {"x": 0, "y": 0, "width": 3008, "height": 1692},
                   "connection": {"hostModel": "Mac16,8", "portType": "usbC", "portNumber": 3, "tunneled": false} }],
    "shortcut": {"keyCode": 18, "modifiers": ["option", "command"], "displayString": "⌥⌘1"},
    "sourceCommand": "displayplacer \"id:… …\" …"
  }],
  "rules": [{
    "id": "UUID", "profileID": "UUID", "isEnabled": true,
    "connectionRequirements": [{ "persistentID": "1D8D03AE-…",
                                 "connection": {"hostModel": "Mac16,8", "portType": "usbC", "portNumber": 3, "tunneled": false} }],
    "triggers": {"launch": true, "displayConnect": true, "wake": true}
  }],
  "knownPorts": [{ "connection": { … }, "name": "Desk Port" }]
}
```

- Array order of `profiles` = user order (menu order and rule tie-break).
- `displays` is metadata for matching and preview; `screens` is what gets applied.
- Decoding: read `version` first. `> current` → refuse ("created by a newer version"). `< current` → step migrations
  (`v1→v2…`). Unknown keys are ignored. Optional fields use `decodeIfPresent`.

### 10.3 Import / export

- Export all (Settings › Data, Manager toolbar menu) or selected profiles (sidebar context menu: includes their rules
  and referenced known ports). Default file name `Display Layouts.json`.
- Import: size limit 5 MB → decode → migrate → **validate everything before changing anything**:
  names 1–100 chars; ≥1 screen; token whitelist; width/height 1…32768; hz 1…1000 or absent; colorDepth 1…32 or absent;
  rotation ∈ {0,90,180,270}; origins within ±100000; exactly one enabled screen at (0,0); unique ids;
  rules reference profiles in the same file; fingerprints well-formed.
- Summary alert: "Import 3 layouts and 2 rules?" → **Add** (new UUIDs on id conflict, name suffix " 2" on name
  conflict, imported rules on conflicting conditions arrive disabled) / **Replace All** (backup first) / Cancel.
- Errors list the first 10 problems with profile names.

---

## 11. Active layout detection (normalization and comparison)

### 11.1 Display identity resolution (`DisplayResolver`)

Map each saved display to at most one live display (bijection), in this order:
1. Same persistent UUID.
2. Saved display is built-in → the single live built-in display.
3. Same `(vendorID, productID, serialNumber)` with `serialNumber != 0`, unique on both sides.
4. Otherwise unresolved.

Also used to rebuild the apply command with current ids and to explain "different monitor connected".

### 11.2 Normalized form

Per screen group (single display or mirror set), keyed by the sorted set of resolved live persistent ids:
`enabled`, and when enabled: `resolution`, `refreshRate`, `colorDepth`, `scaling`, `origin`, `rotation`.

Ignored on purpose: contextual/serial ids, type text, mode numbers, names, argument order, `extraArguments`
(e.g. `quiet:true`), supported mode lists.

### 11.3 Comparison rules (deterministic, pure)

| Field | Rule |
|---|---|
| Display set | Every enabled saved display must resolve and be online. Every online live display must belong to the profile. Extra or missing displays → mismatch |
| Disabled in profile | Matches a live display that is disabled **or** absent |
| Mirror sets | Compared as sets of ids |
| Resolution | Exact |
| Refresh rate | Exact integer. Ignored if either side is absent |
| Color depth | Exact. Ignored if either side is absent |
| Scaling | Exact |
| Rotation | Exact |
| Origin | Each axis within **±2 points** (macOS may nudge edges when snapping; 2 pt is visually immaterial) |
| Main display | Implied by origin (0,0), so already covered |

Result: `.match` or `.mismatch([Difference])` (differences drive verification messages and the Manager's
"Differs from current" hint).

Current layout = the **first profile in user order** that matches. None → `Custom`. displayplacer missing →
`Unknown`. Menu shows a checkmark only on that profile.

Apply verification uses the same comparator restricted to the profile's own displays (a profile that does not mention a
connected display can apply successfully while the current layout still shows `Custom`; documented behavior).

---

## 12. Menu bar behavior

### 12.1 Click handling

```swift
button.sendAction(on: [.leftMouseDown, .rightMouseDown])
@objc func statusItemClicked(_ sender: NSStatusBarButton) {
    let event = NSApp.currentEvent
    let isSecondary = event?.type == .rightMouseDown
        || (event?.type == .leftMouseDown && event?.modifierFlags.contains(.control) == true)
    if isSecondary { systemSettingsOpener.openDisplays() }
    else { statusItem.menu = menu; sender.performClick(nil) }   // menuDidClose → statusItem.menu = nil
}
```

- The menu is attached only for the left-click tracking session, so right click never shows it and native
  highlight/positioning is kept.
- Control-click is treated like right click (macOS convention).
- No event (VoiceOver press) → open the menu.

### 12.2 Open System Settings › Displays (`SystemSettingsOpener`)

1. `x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.Displays-Settings.extension` (macOS 13+; id verified on 27).
2. If `open` returns false: `x-apple.systempreferences:com.apple.preference.displays` (legacy id).
3. Then `/System/Library/PreferencePanes/Displays.prefPane`.
4. Then open System Settings app (`com.apple.systempreferences`).
Each step logged. `NSWorkspace.open` can return true without landing on the right pane; that cannot be detected.

### 12.3 Menu structure (rebuilt in `menuNeedsUpdate`, from cached state)

```
Current Layout: Desk                        (disabled label; "Custom", "Unknown", "Applying TV…")
⚠ Couldn't apply TV – Details…              (only after a failure; opens details alert)
──────────────
Layouts                                     (section header)
  ✓ Desk                          ⌥⌘1
    TV                            ⌥⌘2
    MacBook Only                            (disabled + tooltip when a required display is not connected)
──────────────
Save Current Layout…
Layout Manager…
──────────────
Auto Apply                        ✓
Reapply After Wake                ✓
Launch at Login                   ✓        ("Launch at Login (Needs Approval)" when status = requiresApproval)
──────────────
Displays Settings…                          (same as right click; helps discoverability)
Settings…                         ⌘,
Quit DisplayManager               ⌘Q
```

- Profiles are top-level items (one click to apply) instead of an "Apply Layout ▸" submenu.
- No layouts → disabled "No saved layouts" + Save item stays.
- displayplacer missing → label `displayplacer not found`, item opens Settings › displayplacer; apply items disabled.
- Status icon: `display` template; switches to `display.trianglebadge.exclamationmark` while an automatic apply failure
  is unacknowledged (cleared when the user opens the details or a later apply succeeds).
- Key equivalents on profile items are for display. Duplicate triggers (menu + Carbon hotkey) are deduplicated
  (same profile within 1 s is ignored).

