## 13. UI structure

### 13.1 Save Current Layout…

1. Activate app, capture snapshot. Failure → alert with reason.
2. `NSAlert` "Save Current Layout": summary line per display
   (`Mi Monitor – 3008 × 1692 @ 60 Hz – USB-C Port 3`), accessory: name field (suggestion "Layout N"),
   checkbox "Apply automatically when connected like this" (off by default; creates a rule with the display set and
   the currently identified ports).
3. Empty name → Save disabled. Existing name → second alert "Replace 'Desk'?" (keeps id, rule, shortcut; replaces screens
   and display metadata).
4. Persist, refresh menu; the new profile becomes the current match.

### 13.2 Layout Manager window

`NavigationSplitView`, window ~900×600, frame autosave, reopening brings it to front.

- **Sidebar**: "Current Displays" item; "Layouts" section with profiles (drag to reorder; active one marked with a
  checkmark badge; context menu: Apply, Rename, Duplicate, Export…, Delete…). Toolbar: `+` (Save Current Layout…),
  Import/Export menu.
- **Current Displays** detail: live preview, per display: name, resolution, Hz, scaling, rotation, origin, main,
  identity (persistent/serial ids, EDID vendor/product), connection (`USB-C Port 3 · Desk Port` or reason why unidentified),
  current raw command, "Save as Layout…".
- **Profile** detail:
  - Header: inline editable name, "Active" badge, prominent **Apply Layout** button (disabled with reason if a required
    display is missing), status of last apply.
  - Preview (13.3).
  - Displays list: per screen settings + connected/not connected state.
  - **Auto Apply** section (rule editor, section 14.1).
  - Keyboard shortcut: recorder + Clear.
  - Advanced: "Show Raw Command" disclosure (monospaced, selectable), "Copy Raw Command", "Update From Current Layout…".
  - Menu: Rename, Duplicate, Export…, Delete… (confirm; also removes rules and the hotkey).
- Empty state: "No layouts yet" + Save Current Layout button. First launch (no layouts file) opens the Manager once.

### 13.3 Layout preview (`LayoutPreviewView` + Core `PreviewGeometry`)

- Rectangles from `origin` + size in points. Size from saved `bounds` metadata when present (handles rotation
  correctly), else `resolution`, swapped for 90/270.
- Union bounding box → uniform scale to fit with padding, preserving proportions and relative positions; y down.
- Each rectangle: rounded, adaptive colors; label with name, `3008 × 1692`, `60 Hz`; main display gets a thin
  menu-bar strip on top (like System Settings); rotation badge (`90°`); mirror set shows `Mirrored` + member names;
  not-connected display drawn dashed with a warning glyph. Details hide progressively when the rectangle is small.
- Disabled screens listed below the canvas as chips ("Disabled: Built-in Retina Display").
- Accessibility label per display.

### 13.4 Settings window (`NSTabViewController`, toolbar style)

| Tab | Content |
|---|---|
| General | Launch at Login (with "Needs approval → Open Login Items" when required), Auto Apply, Reapply After Wake |
| Automation | "Wait for displays after wake" (0–10 s, default 2), Learned Ports table (rename, delete; technical label shown), Recent Activity list, Copy Diagnostics |
| displayplacer | Status (Installed / not found / unsupported version), path, version, Choose…, Reset, Re-check, install instructions |
| Data | Import…, Export…, Show Data Folder in Finder |

---

## 14. Auto-apply logic

### 14.1 Rule model and editor

- One rule per profile in the UI (model allows more).
- Implicit condition (always): the connected display set equals the profile's display set (11.3 display-set rule).
  This is the "External monitor connected" condition and blocks another monitor on the same port from matching.
- Optional per external display: "uses [Any port | Desk Port (USB-C Port 3) | …]" + **Learn Current Port**.
  This is the "Matching physical connection detected" condition.
- Triggers: App launch, Display connection, Wake from sleep (per rule). Effective trigger = global toggle AND rule toggle
  (Auto Apply gates launch + connect; Reapply After Wake gates wake).
- Conflict warning shown in the editor when another enabled rule has identical conditions.

### 14.2 Rule matching (`RuleMatcher`, pure)

```
candidates = enabled rules whose trigger set contains the trigger
           ∩ display-set condition holds against the snapshot
           ∩ every connection requirement: resolved live display's fingerprint == required fingerprint
             (unidentified port never satisfies a requirement)
winner     = max(number of connection requirements)   // most specific wins
             tie → earliest profile in user order
```

### 14.3 Decision and loop prevention (`AutomationPolicy`, pure)

State: `lastStableSignature`, `lastAutoApply (signature, profileID, date)`, `recentAutoApplies [Date]`,
`suppressUntil`, `preSleep (signature, matchedProfileID)`, `paused`.

1. **Connect trigger fires only when the topology signature changed** since the last stable snapshot.
   Mode/origin changes (the user editing Displays settings, or our own apply) never trigger auto-apply.
2. **Self-induced changes**: events between apply start and apply end + 3 s are absorbed. The post-apply signature
   becomes `lastStableSignature` without evaluating rules (covers `enabled:false` making a display go offline).
3. **Already correct**: if the current layout already matches the target → no apply, just record.
4. **Once per topology entry**: after an auto-apply for signature S, no further auto-apply for S until the signature
   changes (unplug/replug resets it; wake re-evaluates, see 5).
5. **Wake**: always re-evaluate the stable snapshot. Apply only if the target does not match the current layout.
   Target = matching wake rule; if none and Reapply After Wake is on and the signature equals the pre-sleep signature →
   the profile that matched before sleep.
6. **Circuit breaker**: more than 3 automatic applies in 2 minutes → pause automation, show warning in menu, resume on the
   next topology change after the pause window or when the user toggles Auto Apply.
7. Session not active (fast user switching, login window) → skip; re-evaluate on `sessionDidBecomeActive` (as wake).

---

## 15. Display connect and wake handling (`AutomationCoordinator`)

```
CG reconfig callback ─┐   (flags: add/remove/enabled/disabled → topology; others → layout only)
IOKit DP transport    ├─► mark dirty(reason) ─► restart debounce task (cancel previous)
first-match/terminated┘
NSWorkspace didWake / screensDidWake ─► reason = wake, extra initial delay = settleDelay (default 2 s)
App launch ─► reason = launch (once)

debounce: 1.0 s of quiet
stability: capture signature every 0.75 s until 2 consecutive identical signatures, and every external display
           is either port-identified or has no pending transport; max 8 s (connect/launch) or 15 s (wake)
           timeout → log "displays not ready", refresh status only
refresh AppModel.snapshot + current layout (always)
decide via AutomationPolicy (section 14.3) → ApplyCoordinator.apply(profile, .automatic(reason))
willSleep → cancel pending automation tasks, record preSleep state
```

- Nothing blocks the main thread; all waits are `Task.sleep` in cancellable tasks.
- CG callbacks arrive in begin/end pairs; only end-of-configuration events count.

---

## 16. Apply pipeline (`Core.LayoutApplier` + `ApplyCoordinator`)

```
1. locate displayplacer                         (missing → executableUnavailable)
2. capture snapshot                             (malformed → error)
3. resolve profile displays → live displays     (missing enabled display → displayNotConnected;
                                                 different external present → "Mi Monitor is not connected. Connected: Dell U2720Q")
4. pre-check supported modes (same rotation)    (→ unsupportedMode, not retryable)
5. current already matches?                     (→ alreadyApplied, no process run)
6. run displayplacer with resolved arguments
7. classify output (section 9.4)
8. verify: wait 0.75 s, list again, compare (profile displays only); mismatch → re-check once after 1.5 s
9. retry per policy or finish
```

| Policy | Attempts | Delays between attempts | Retries on |
|---|---|---|---|
| Automatic (connect/wake/launch) | 4 | 1.5 s, 3 s, 5 s | displayNotConnected (still waking), displayNotFound, verification mismatch, timeout (once), failed |
| Manual (menu, hotkey, Manager) | 2 | 1.5 s | displayNotFound, verification mismatch, timeout |

- Bounded: no infinite loops, total automatic time ≈ 10 s + process time.
- Final failure → manual: `NSAlert` with message + "Show Details" (stdout/stderr, command, differences);
  automatic: menu warning item + icon badge + diagnostics entry. No notification permission needed.
- Every attempt logged (attempt n/N, reason, exit code, duration).

---

## 17. Launch at Login

- `SMAppService.mainApp.register()` / `unregister()`; toggle state = `status == .enabled`
  (`.requiresApproval` shown as on + "Needs Approval" with a button calling `openSystemSettingsLoginItems()`).
- Status re-read on menu open, when Settings appears, and on `didBecomeActive`. Errors shown in an alert.
- If the app is not running from `/Applications`, Settings shows a hint (registration is path-bound).
- Not enabled automatically. Phase 2 verification will not toggle it (persistent system configuration); the user does.

---

## 18. Keyboard shortcuts

Decision: **Carbon `RegisterEventHotKey`** (no Accessibility or Input Monitoring permission; the standard mechanism used by
shortcut utilities). `NSEvent.addGlobalMonitorForEvents` needs Accessibility and cannot consume keys, so it is rejected.

- Optional per profile, none by default. Stored as `keyCode` (layout-independent) + modifiers + display string.
- Recorder: `NSViewRepresentable` field that captures `keyDown`/`flagsChanged` while focused; Esc cancels, Delete clears.
  All hotkeys are temporarily unregistered while recording so an existing shortcut can be re-recorded.
- Validation (`ShortcutValidator`, pure): must include ⌘, ⌥ or ⌃; not a duplicate of another profile.
- Conflict checks before accepting:
  1. `CopySymbolicHotKeys`: reject if it equals an enabled macOS system shortcut ("Used by macOS").
  2. Probe with `kEventHotKeyExclusive`: `eventHotKeyExistsErr` → reject ("Used by another app"). On success, immediately
     unregister and re-register **non-exclusive**, so we never block other apps.
- Registered non-exclusively → we never override other apps' hotkeys.
- Limitation: apps using event taps or non-Carbon mechanisms cannot be detected; if such an app uses the same combination,
  both respond. The UI states this in one line. Probe semantics across processes come from header docs and are verified in
  Phase 2; if the probe cannot detect other processes, only check 1 remains and this is documented.

---

## 19. Error handling

| Case | Detection | User-facing result |
|---|---|---|
| displayplacer missing | locator | Menu label, Settings status with install instructions, apply/save disabled |
| Unsupported displayplacer version | `--version` | Settings status "Update displayplacer" |
| Malformed list output | parser | Alert "Couldn't read the current display configuration" + details |
| Saved display not connected | resolver | Menu item disabled with tooltip; apply error names the missing display |
| Monitor changed | resolver (different external present) | Error names expected and connected monitors |
| Unsupported resolution | mode pre-check / output | "3008 × 1692 @ 60 Hz is not supported by Mi Monitor" (no retry) |
| Apply failure | exit code / verification | Alert or menu warning with details |
| Display still waking | stability timeout / displayNotFound | Automatic retries; final message "Displays were not ready" |
| Topology detection failure | IOKit read failure / class missing | Ports show "Not available"; port rules cannot match; identity rules still work |
| Connection not identifiable | join result | Rule editor explains why; Learn Current Port shows reason |
| Import failure | decode/validate | Alert listing problems; nothing changed |
| Persistence failure | write error | Alert; in-memory state kept; retry on next change |
| Launch at Login failure | SMAppService error | Alert with system message; toggle reflects real status |
| Hotkey unavailable | validation/registration | Inline message in recorder |

All errors are `DisplayManagerError: LocalizedError` with `userMessage` + `diagnosticDetail`. No force unwraps on hardware
or file data. Hardware changes never crash the app.

---

## 20. Logging and diagnostics

- `os.Logger(subsystem: "com.bachana.DisplayManager", category:)` with categories:
  `app`, `displays`, `topology`, `displayplacer`, `automation`, `apply`, `persistence`, `hotkeys`, `login`.
- Logged (event-driven only, no polling, so no spam): display connected/disconnected, snapshot summary, topology
  fingerprints, matched rule, apply attempt/retry/failure/success with duration, wake/sleep handling, circuit breaker.
- Hardware identifiers logged with default (private) privacy; non-identifying values public.
- `DiagnosticsLog` keeps the last 300 entries in memory for Settings › Automation › Recent Activity.
- "Copy Diagnostics": app version, macOS version, host model, displayplacer path/version, current snapshot
  (displays, ports, current command), last apply attempts with stdout/stderr, recent events.
- README documents: `log stream --predicate 'subsystem == "com.bachana.DisplayManager"' --level info`.

---

## 21. Installation and build strategy

### 21.1 `Scripts/build-app.sh [debug|release]`

`set -euo pipefail`, resolves its own dir, selects toolchain (section 3), `swift build`, assembles bundle into
`$DM_BUILD_DIR/dist/DisplayManager.app`, writes version (`CFBundleShortVersionString` from a `VERSION` variable,
`CFBundleVersion` = UTC timestamp), `plutil -lint`, `codesign --force --sign - --options runtime`,
`codesign --verify --strict --verbose=2`, prints the bundle path.

### 21.2 `install.sh`

```
Usage: ./install.sh [--no-launch] [--help]
```
1. `set -euo pipefail`; resolve script dir with `cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd -P`.
2. Verify macOS ≥ 14, `codesign`, `plutil`, `ditto` exist, and a working Swift toolchain (Xcode or CLT). If none:
   print `xcode-select --install` (or "accept the Xcode license") and exit 1. Never runs `sudo`.
3. Check displayplacer (PATH, `/opt/homebrew/bin`, `/usr/local/bin`). Missing → clear warning + `brew install displayplacer`;
   installation continues (the app reports the missing dependency). Never installs Homebrew or displayplacer.
4. Build Release via `Scripts/build-app.sh release`.
5. If DisplayManager is running, quit it (`pkill -x DisplayManager`, wait up to 5 s). State is saved on every change, so
   nothing is lost.
6. Replace safely: `ditto` into `/Applications/.DisplayManager.app.installing`, verify signature there, remove the old
   `/Applications/DisplayManager.app`, `mv` the new one into place. Executable permissions preserved by `ditto`.
   `/Applications` not writable → clear error (no sudo).
7. Verify installed bundle: executable bit, `codesign --verify`, Info.plist bundle id.
8. Print final location; launch with `open` unless `--no-launch`.

Ad-hoc signing notes (README): locally built apps carry no quarantine attribute, so Gatekeeper does not block them.
Copying the app to another Mac via download adds quarantine → right-click › Open once. After a reinstall, macOS may treat
the new ad-hoc signature as a new app for Login Items; if the toggle shows off, enable it again.

---

## 22. Testing strategy

### 22.1 Unit tests (Swift Testing, `swift test`, hostless, no hardware)

| Area | Cases |
|---|---|
| ScreenSpecCodec | full spec, negative origins, mirror `A+B`, `enabled:false` only, unknown tokens kept, whitespace, round trip |
| ListOutputParser | real fixture (2 displays), main-display suffix, rotation hint suffix, "current mode" marker, synthetic mirrored/disabled/clamshell/3-display fixtures, missing command line, garbage input |
| CommandBuilder | argv exactness, id re-resolution, disabled screens, whitelist rejection, raw-command quoting with spaces in path |
| ExecutableLocator | order, override, PATH parsing, non-executable skipped, version parsing and minimum check |
| ApplyOutcome | classification table in 9.4 |
| EDID | vendor PNP decode ("XMI"), product 10145, serial 19, name descriptor "Mi Monitor", bad header/short data → nil |
| SnapshotAssembler | join by EDID triple, builtin, no transport, ambiguous identical monitors, HDMI inactive node ignored, tunneled flag, fallback property derivation |
| ConnectionFingerprint | key format, equality, host model difference, labels |
| DisplayResolver | UUID, builtin fallback, serial fallback, serial 0 not used, bijection |
| LayoutComparator | identical, origin tolerance ±2 boundary, res/hz/scaling/rotation diffs, extra/missing display, disabled vs absent, mirror order, depth ignored when absent, differences list |
| RuleMatcher | display set, port requirement, other monitor on same port rejected, specificity, tie by order, disabled rules, trigger filter, unidentified port |
| AutomationPolicy | connect only on signature change, self-induced suppression, already-correct no-op, once per topology entry, replug re-applies, wake re-evaluation and pre-sleep fallback, circuit breaker, inactive session |
| LayoutApplier (fake DisplayControl + instant Sleeper) | success, verification retry, bounded attempts, non-retryable stop, already-applied short-circuit, missing display waits |
| LayoutDocument | round trip, newer version refused, unknown keys ignored, migration hook, corrupt data |
| ImportValidator | each validation rule, Add merge (id/name conflicts, disabled conflicting rules), Replace |
| ShortcutValidator, PreviewGeometry | modifiers required, duplicates; fit/scale math, rotation swap, negative origins |

Fixtures from this Mac use anonymized ids. Synthetic fixtures are labeled as synthetic in their file header.

### 22.2 Build and static validation

- `swift build -c release -Xswiftc -warnings-as-errors` (Swift 6 strict concurrency).
- `swift test`.
- `plutil -lint` on the assembled Info.plist; `codesign --verify --strict`.
- `bash -n install.sh Scripts/build-app.sh` (no shellcheck installed).

### 22.3 Phase 2 on-machine verification (safe)

- Run `install.sh`, confirm `/Applications/DisplayManager.app` exists, is signed, launches (`pgrep -x DisplayManager`).
- Check logs / Copy Diagnostics output: expect Mi Monitor identified as `USB-C Port 3`.
- Only a **same-layout** apply may be exercised (expected result `alreadyApplied`, no visible change). Switching to a
  different layout, Launch at Login and port moves are left to the user's manual QA.

### 22.4 Manual QA (user)

Left click menu / right click Displays / ctrl-click; save layout; apply between two different layouts; rename, duplicate,
delete, reorder; preview accuracy; Learn Current Port on two different ports; unplug/replug → auto-apply once; same monitor
on another port → other layout; different monitor on the learned port → no apply; sleep/wake reapply; Launch at Login
toggle and reboot; shortcuts incl. conflict messages; import/export round trip and invalid file; displayplacer missing
(temporary path override to a non-existent file).

---

## 23. Phase 2 execution order

1. Scaffold: `Package.swift`, `.gitignore`, `Packaging/Info.plist`, `Scripts/build-app.sh`; confirm CLT build works.
2. Core models, ScreenSpecCodec, ListOutputParser, CommandBuilder, ExecutableLocator + tests.
3. EDID, PortTransportRecord, SnapshotAssembler, DisplayResolver, LayoutComparator, RuleMatcher + tests.
4. AutomationPolicy, LayoutApplier, LayoutDocument, ImportValidator, ShortcutValidator, PreviewGeometry + tests.
5. System adapters: DisplayplacerClient, CGDisplayReader, PortTopologyReader, SnapshotService, DiagnosticsLog.
6. AppModel, ProfileStore, Preferences, StatusItemController/menu, SystemSettingsOpener, MainMenu.
7. Save flow, ApplyCoordinator, Layout Manager (list, detail, preview, current displays, raw command).
8. Rule editor + Learn Current Port + known ports; AutomationCoordinator + DisplayEventMonitor.
9. Settings window, LoginItemController, import/export.
10. HotKeyCenter + recorder.
11. App icon, accessibility labels, empty states, README.
12. `install.sh`; full verification (22.2, 22.3); final report.

---

## 24. Known limitations and risks

| # | Risk / limitation | Type | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Port detection uses IORegistry classes/properties (`IOPortTransportStateDisplayPort`, `ParentBuiltInPortNumber`, …) that are readable with public IOKit APIs but are **not a documented contract**. They can change in a future macOS | Fact | Fallback derivation chain (8.1), graceful "Not available", diagnostics |
| 2 | Verified only on this Mac (M4 Pro, macOS 27), direct USB-C DP Alt Mode. Thunderbolt docks (tunneled DP), MST/daisy chains, HDMI with a connected display, Intel Macs, macOS 14/15 not verified | Fact | Defensive handling (8.4); identity-only rules keep working |
| 3 | macOS gives receptacle numbers, not physical positions. The app cannot say "left/right" | Fact | Custom port names |
| 4 | Persistent UUID staying identical across ports is expected but not verified here (would need a cable move) | Uncertain | Resolver falls back to EDID serial; ids re-resolved at apply time |
| 5 | Two identical monitors with identical EDID serials cannot be told apart for port mapping | Fact | Marked ambiguous; port rules for them never match |
| 6 | displayplacer uses private CoreGraphics APIs; a macOS update can break it. Parser targets the v1.4.0 format. `enabled:false` may require a replug to re-enable. Rotating the built-in display can crash (displayplacer warns) | Fact | Version check, clear errors, verification after apply |
| 7 | Rotated-display size semantics in displayplacer `res` not verified | Uncertain | Preview uses saved CG bounds |
| 8 | Carbon hotkeys are a legacy (but supported) API; conflict detection is partial | Fact + inference | Non-exclusive registration, system shortcut check, exclusive probe (verified in Phase 2) |
| 9 | Ad-hoc signing: Login Items may need re-enabling after a reinstall; distribution to other Macs needs right-click › Open | Inference | Documented; toggle always shows real status |
| 10 | Xcode license not accepted: build depends on Command Line Tools Swift ≥ 6.0 | Fact | install.sh detects toolchain and explains the fix |
| 11 | Repo lives in iCloud Drive. "Optimize Mac Storage" can evict source files and break builds | Fact | Build dir outside iCloud; README note (`brctl download` or keep files local) |
| 12 | Displays deep link is undocumented; `NSWorkspace.open` may return true without reaching the pane | Fact | 4-step fallback chain; verified id exists on macOS 27 |
| 13 | Temporary `statusItem.menu` + `performClick` pattern relies on AppKit behavior, not a documented left/right API | Common practice | Isolated in StatusItemController; fallback `menu.popUp(positioning:at:in:)` |
| 14 | Debounce/settle timings are heuristics; slow monitors may need more time | Inference | Wake delay setting; bounded retries |
| 15 | Clamshell and mirroring follow displayplacer semantics; tested with synthetic fixtures only | Fact | Unit tests + manual QA |

---

## 25. Assumptions to confirm (change any before Phase 2 if needed)

1. Product name **DisplayManager**, bundle id **com.bachana.DisplayManager**.
2. Minimum macOS **14.0**.
3. **SwiftPM + Command Line Tools** build instead of an Xcode project (no Xcode license needed).
4. Auto Apply and Reapply After Wake default **on**, Launch at Login default **off**.
5. Profiles listed **directly in the menu** (one click) instead of an "Apply Layout" submenu.
6. Control-click behaves like right-click.
7. No git repository will be initialized unless requested (only `.gitignore` is added).

---

## 26. Deviations log (Phase 2)

Small adjustments made during Phase 2. No requirement was dropped.

| # | Area (plan section) | Change | Reason |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Toolchain (3) | Scripts share `Scripts/toolchain.sh` (selection) and `Scripts/swift.sh` (wrapper for `swift build/test` with the build dir outside iCloud). `xcrun swift` is used first; CLT is the fallback | The Xcode license is accepted on this Mac now, so `xcrun` works. The CLT fallback stays for Macs without Xcode |
| 2 | Toolchain (3) | When the CLT has stale `*.private.swiftinterface` files for PackageDescription, the script builds a symlink mirror without them inside the build dir and sets `SWIFTPM_CUSTOM_LIBS_DIR` | The CLT on this Mac failed to link `Package.swift` ("Undefined symbols … Package.__allocating_init"). No files outside the build dir are touched |
| 3 | Package (3, 5) | `swift-tools-version: 6.0` with `swiftLanguageModes: [.v6]` | Swift 6 strict concurrency for the whole package, as planned |
| 4 | App icon (3) | `Scripts/generate-icon.swift` renders every iconset size itself, `Scripts/generate-icon.sh` runs it and `iconutil`. No `sips` step | Rendering each size directly is sharper than downscaling and needs one tool less |
| 5 | displayplacer (9) | `ProcessRunner` runs displayplacer with a fixed minimal environment (`PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin`, `LANG`) | Predictable output; no user shell configuration involved |
| 6 | displayplacer (9, 20) | displayplacer status is logged only when it changes | Re-checks on every Settings open and menu refresh produced repeated identical log lines |
| 7 | Persistence (10) | Loading `layouts.json` decodes the document without the stricter import validation | The app's own file must always load; an unreadable file is still moved aside |
| 8 | Port detection (8) | "Not available" is decided by checking for the base transport class when no DisplayPort transport records exist | An empty result on a supported Mac (no external display) must not look like "unsupported" |
| 9 | Automation (15) | Topology events that arrive while an apply is running are not evaluated | The apply's own result updates the snapshot and the policy, so half-applied states are never evaluated |
| 10 | Hotkeys (18) | Probe semantics verified on macOS 27: the exclusive probe detects shortcuts another app registered exclusively; non-exclusive registrations and event taps cannot be detected. Recording suspends all hotkeys and resumes them when recording ends | Required by 18; the UI states the limitation in one line |
| 11 | Menu (12.3) | Explicit "Automation paused after repeated applies – Resume" item; "Launch at Login (Needs Approval)" opens Login Items | Makes the paused state and the approval step actionable from the menu |
| 12 | Profiles (13.2) | Duplicate copies screens and display metadata only, not the Auto Apply rule or the shortcut | A copy with the same rule would conflict with the original; shortcuts must be unique |
| 13 | Save flow (13.1) | After saving with "Apply automatically…", an info alert names any rule with the same conditions | Conflicts would otherwise only be visible in the rule editor |
| 14 | Settings (13.4) | Fixed size per tab; General also shows the version; Data shows counts and the file path | Stable window layout with the toolbar tab style |
| 15 | App lifecycle | Reopening the app (Finder, Spotlight, `open`) shows the Layout Manager | A menu bar app otherwise gives no feedback when opened again |
| 16 | Installer (21.2) | Running copies are found with `pgrep -x` and filtered by executable path, then SIGTERM, wait 5 s, SIGKILL. The old app is moved to `.DisplayManager.app.previous` and restored if the swap fails. Extra checks: directories owned by another user in the old bundle, and iCloud-evicted source files | Safer than `pkill -x` (never touches another app with the same name) and never leaves `/Applications` without an app |
| 17 | Build (21.1) | Ad-hoc signing adds `--timestamp=none` | No timestamp server is used for ad-hoc signatures |
| 18 | Text | A shared `counted(_:_:)` helper gives "1 layout" / "2 layouts" in alerts, Settings and log messages | Replaces "layout(s)" wording |
| 19 | Menu bar (12), logging (20) | The status item logs once after launch whether it is visible, with a hint about a full menu bar or System Settings › Menu Bar › Allow in the Menu Bar | On macOS 27 status item windows are hosted remotely and do not appear in `CGWindowList` (verified with a minimal probe app), so visibility can only be checked in-process |
| 20 | Toolchain (3) | Selection now tries the active developer dir, then any Xcode in `/Applications` or `~/Applications`, then the CLT. A candidate whose SDK declares SwiftUI's `@State` as a macro but has no `libSwiftUIMacros.dylib` next to it is skipped in the first pass; the second pass uses its previous macOS SDK via `SDKROOT` (process only) and prints a note. Failure lists every candidate checked | `install.sh` failed on a CLT-only Mac: the macOS 27 SDK declares `macro State()` (SwiftUIMacros), and that plugin ships only with Xcode. The CLT also install the macOS 26.5 SDK, where `State` is a plain property wrapper. Verified: CLT + 26.5 release build with warnings as errors, `install.sh`, and the app launching |
| 21 | Toolchain (3) | With the CLT, `Scripts/swift.sh build/test` adds `-Xswiftc -plugin-path` for the CLT's `plugins/testing` folder | SwiftPM from the CLT does not load Swift Testing's `TestingMacros` plugin by itself, with any SDK. Xcode is unchanged |
